es>...)?', ...)\n```\n\nThe word-based parsing: '5 minutes' \u2192 5*60 = 300. Yes.\n\nparse_duration('1 hour') \u2192 3600. Yes.\n\nparse_duration('10 minutes') \u2192 600. Yes.\n\nOK. Now `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')`:\n\nyoutube-dl's unified_timestamp:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    ...\n    pm = ...\n    ...\n    # TODO: timezone offsets\n    ...\n    for expr in (...)\n```\n\nIt builds `datetime` and returns `calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())` \u2014 UTC. For '2019-01-01' it should parse via one of the date formats. There's handling: `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$`? The function tries `datetime.datetime.strptime(date_str, format)` for formats in `_DATE_FORMATS`... Hmm, actually unified_timestamp uses regexes + month names. ISO 'YYYY-MM-DD' is handled: there's a branch:\n\n```python\n    m = re.search(r'(?P<year>\\d{4})-(?P<month>\\d{1,2})-(?P<day>\\d{1,2})', date_str)\n```\n\nHmm, not exactly, but ISO dates are widely used in youtube-dl tests with unified_timestamp, e.g. 'upload_date': '20191231'... Those are dates not timestamps. But unified_timestamp('2019-01-01') \u2014 I'm fairly sure returns 1546300800. Many extractors pass ISO dates to unified_timestamp. OK.\n\nTo be safe I'll compare with `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` and also assert `is not None`.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually there's a subtlety with unified_timestamp and day_first for numeric dates: '2019-01-01' is unambiguous (year first). Fine.\n\nNow, let me also consider: in entry B, 'created_on': '2019-01-02' \u2192 unified_timestamp \u2192 1546387200. Compare with computed. Good.\n\nNow finalize after get_info.\n\nAlso \u2014 the description chain: `item.get('md_desc') or clean_html(item.get('description')) or clean_html(item.get('text'))`. For entry A: md_desc='MD desc A' \u2192 description='MD desc A'. For B: no md_desc \u2192 clean_html('<i>Desc B</i>') \u2192 'Desc