SS \u2192 minutes=6, seconds=00 \u2192 360. But wait, could it match hours=6, minutes=0, seconds=... no, there's no second colon. So 360. \u2713.\n\nActually hold on, let me re-check youtube-dl's parse_duration more carefully:\n\n```python\ndef parse_duration(s):\n    if s is None:\n        return None\n\n    s = s.strip()\n\n    days, hours, mins, secs, ms = [None] * 5\n\n    m = re.match(r'(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):(?P<minutes>[0-9]{1,2}):(?P<seconds>[0-9]{1,2}(?:\\.[0-9]+)?)(?:\\.(?P<ms>[0-9]{1,3})0*)?$', s)\n    if m:\n        days, hours, mins, secs, ms = m.groups()\n    else:\n        m = re.match(r'(?:(?P<hours>[0-9]+)[hH])?(?:\\s*(?P<minutes>[0-9]+)\\s*[mM])?(?:\\s*(?P<seconds>[0-9]+)\\s*[sS])?', s)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember the exact implementation. Let me check via get_info(\"parse_duration\"). It's in the batch.\n\nActually the real youtube-dl parse_duration:\n\n```python\ndef parse_duration(s):\n    if s is None:\n        return None\n\n    s = s.strip()\n\n    days, hours, mins, secs, ms = [None] * 5\n\n    m = re.match(r'(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):(?P<minutes>[0-9]{1,2}):(?P<seconds>[0-9]{1,2}(?:\\.[0-9]+)?)(?:\\.(?P<ms>[0-9]{1,3})0*)?$', s)\n    if m:\n        days, hours, mins, secs, ms = m.groups()\n    else:\n        m = re.match(r'(?:(?P<hours>[0-9]+):)?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{1,2}):(?P<seconds>[0-9]{1,2}(?:\\.[0-9]+)?)(?:\\.(?P<ms>[0-9]{1,3})0*)?$', s)\n        if m:\n            hours, mins, secs, ms = m.groups()\n        else:\n            m = re.match(r'(?P<seconds>[0-9]+)(?:\\.(?P<ms>[0-9]+))?$', s)\n            if m:\n                secs, ms = m.groups()\n            else:\n                ...\n                # word-based\n                for duration, regex in [...]:\n```\n\nHmm, whatever \u2014 '6:00' \u2192 360 per the second regex. I'm confident. But let me verify via get_info anyway.\n\nAlso '5 minutes' \u2014 word-based: `(?P<minutes>\\d+)\\s*minutes?` \u2192 300. \u2713\n\n'1 hour' \u2192 3600 \u2713. '10 minutes' \u2192 600 \u2713.\n\nOK. Now let me also double check `unified_timestamp` via get_info.\n\nNow, let me think about whether `_real_extract` might be affected by `self._downloader` being None in `_parse_json`... `_parse_json`:\n\n```python\ndef _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True):\n    try:\n        ...\n    except ...\n        if fatal:\n            raise ExtractorError(...)\n        else:\n            self.report_warning('Failed to parse JSON' ...)\n            return None\n```\n\nOnly on failure. Our JSONs are valid. Fine.\n\nAlright \u2014 also, should the tests call `ie.extract(url)` instead? No, `_real_extract` directly.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one more consideration. The problem statement says \"lines 155-243 do not execute\" \u2014 meaning the whole `_real_extract`. My tests call `_real_extract` directly, covering those lines. \n\nNow, let me also consider adding a test for the course path where `_search_regex` for module fails \u2192 ExtractorError? Not needed for coverage of the listed lines (the regex search line is covered by success). Skip.\n\nLet me also think about `pytest` import \u2014 I don't use pytest.raises; only fixtures. So no `import pytest` needed. But some linters want it... I'll skip it. Actually, wait \u2014 should I guard against `monkeypatch` fixture? It's provided by pytest automatically. Fine.\n\nNow, let me make the get_info calls.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether patching at class level with monkeypatch could interfere with `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 no.\n\nAnd whether creating `LinuxAcademyIE()` has side effects \u2014 InfoExtractor.__init__ sets _downloader=None, _cookie_jar=None, maybe _geo_bypass... Fine.\n\nLet me also double check that `LinuxAcademyIE` in the actual module doesn't override `_real_extract` differently or have `_LOGIN_URL` etc. The snippet is authoritative for lines 155-243.\n\nOne more subtle thing: the snippet shows `chapter_id, l