ex(..., 'lesson', default='{}')` returns '{}' \u2192 `_parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False)` \u2192 {} \u2192 falsy \u2192 skip. Then title fallback `_search_regex(..., 'title', group='value')` \u2014 must match, else raises ExtractorError. Our webpage will contain `>Lecture: Foo` so it matches.\n\nNow `_parse_json` \u2014 real. It might call `self._downloader`? No, it just parses. Fine.\n\n`_extract_m3u8_formats` \u2014 monkeypatched.\n\n`_download_webpage` \u2014 monkeypatched.\n\nNow, is there anything in `_real_extract` that touches `self._downloader`? No, given our patches.\n\nNow the `_VALID_URL`. Let me fetch it.\n\nAlso note: the code snippet at top shows imports `import re`, `from youtube_dl.compat import compat_str`, `from youtube_dl.utils import ...`. The class in the real module imports from `..compat` and `..utils`. The test will import the real class.\n\nPotential issue: `try_get` \u2014 real util. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about `chapter_number` for entries: it's an int (1, 2). Assert accordingly.\n\nNow, `item_id` for course path = course_id. For single video = '%s-%s' % (chapter_id, lecture_id).\n\nLet me now write out the test file:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import clean_html, parse_duration, unified_timestamp, urljoin\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage):\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', lambda url, video_id, *args, **kwargs: webpage)\n    return ie\n```\n\nWait, but monkeypatch.setattr on instance for a method that exists on class: monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) \u2014 it records the old value (which is found via getattr on the instance \u2192 the class method) and on undo it does `setattr(obj, name, old)`? Actually monkeypatch.setattr with an instance: it does `oldval = getattr(target, name)` then sets. On undo, it restores by setattr \u2014 which would set the bound method as an instance attribute, leaving pollution! Hmm.\n\nLet me check pytest monkeypatch semantics: `monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value, raising=True)`. The undo: `if name in self._setattr: ...` it stores `(obj, name, value)` and on undo does `setattr(obj, name, oldval)` \u2014 wait no.\n\nLooking at pytest source (_pytest/monkeypatch.py):\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nand undo:\n\n```python\nfor obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n    if value is not notset:\n        setattr(obj, name, value)\n    else:\n        delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance where the attribute doesn't exist in instance `__dict__` but exists on the class: `oldval = getattr(target, name)` \u2192 the bound method (notset? no, it's found via getattr). Then on undo, `setattr(obj, name, bound_method)` \u2014 this sets an instance attribute pointing to the bound method of the original instance... wait, the bound method is bound to `ie` itself. So after undo, `ie._download_webpage` would be an instance attribute = bound method. That's pollution of that instance, but the instance is local to the test and garbage collected. Since each test creates its own IE instance, there's no cross-test pollution. The class attribute is untouched. So it's fine.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `if inspect.isclass(target): oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)`. For instance target, oldval = getattr(target, name, notset) \u2192 bound method (exists). So undo does setattr(instance, name, bound_method). Instance-level pollution only, instance dies with test. OK, acceptable. \n\nAlternatively, use `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', ...)` on the class \u2014 monkeypatch restores properly for classes (uses `target.__dict__`). But patching the class affects all instances \u2014 fine since we create instances within the test. But if the patch is class-level and two tests run... monkeypatch undoes after each test. Class-level patching is cleaner for restoration. But then the fake function receives `self` as first arg when called as `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. So signature: `def fake_download(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs): return webpage`.\n\nI'll do class-level patching via monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake) \u2014 clean restore.\n\nHmm, but careful: `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 since it's a class, oldval = target.__dict__.get(name) \u2192 the original function \u2192 restored via setattr \u2192 back to normal function (unbound). Good, no pollution.\n\nLet me write helper:\n\n```python\ndef _patch_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    downloaded = {}\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded['url'] = url\n        downloaded['video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n\n    def fake_extract_m3u8(self, m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n        return [{\n            'format_id': 'hls-test',\n            'url': m3u8_url,\n            'ext': ext,\n            'protocol': kwargs.get('entry_protocol'),\n            'm3u8_id': kwargs.get('m3u8_id'),\n        }]\n\n    def fake_sort(self, formats=None, field_preference=None):\n        fake_sort.called = True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_sort_formats', fake_sort)\n    return downloaded\n```\n\nHmm, `fake_sort.called` \u2014 function attribute; fine.\n\nNow the course test:\n\n```python\ndef test_real_extract_course(monkeypatch):\n    course_url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/course/dashboard/my-course'  # depends on _VALID_URL\n    module = {...}\n    webpage = '<html>window.module = %s;</html>' % json.dumps(module)\n    ...\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    patch...\n    result = ie._real_extract(course_url)  # or ie.extract(course_url)?\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 should I call `_real_extract` directly or `extract`? `extract` does more (calls `_real_extract` after checks). The lines in question are in `_real_extract`. Calling `ie._real_extract(url)` directly is simplest and avoids `extract`'s extra machinery (like `_match_id`, suitable checks). But `extract` also handles `_type: playlist` via process... Actually `extract` returns the raw result of `_real_extract` (the ie_result), and playlist processing happens later in YoutubeDL. So `extract` would work too. But `extract` calls `self._match_entry`? No... `extract`:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    ...\n    self._match_valid_url? \n    ie_result = self._real_extract(url)\n    ...\n    return ie_result\n```\n\nIt also does `self.report_extraction`? Something like that which may need downloader. To be safe, call `_real_extract` directly. The coverage target is `_real_extract` lines anyway.\n\nHmm, but is calling a private method OK for a test? It's common in these coverage-driven exercises. Yes.\n\nNow, the URL for course path. I need _VALID_URL. Let me guess from the real youtube-dl source. I'm fairly confident the real file is:\n\n```python\nclass LinuxAcademyIE(InfoExtractor):\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n        https?://\n            (?:\n                linuxacademy\\.com/|\n                (?:www\\.)?app\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n            )\n        (?:\n            course/dashboard/(?P<course_id>[^/?#&]+)|\n            chapter/(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#&]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#&]+)\n        )\n    '''\n```\n\nHmm wait, but then `chapter_id` group would capture e.g. '