    for regex in ...:\n    ...\n```\n\nIt has special handling: `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$`? There's `_DATE_FORMATS`... I'm fairly confident ISO dates work. To be extra safe, use a fuller format like '2019-01-01 12:00:00' or 'January 1, 2019'. Hmm. unified_timestamp handles many formats including 'YYYY-MM-DD'. I'll trust it but compute expected via unified_timestamp in the test so the assertion is robust even if the exact epoch differs.\n\nActually, hmm, there's a subtlety: unified_timestamp('2019-01-01') might interpret with timezone UTC \u2192 1546300800. If it used local time it'd vary, but youtube-dl parses dates as UTC by default (uses `datetime` with timezone.utc or calendar.timegm). Comparing to `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` in the test avoids any issue.\n\nFor created_on '2019-01-02' similarly.\n\nNow the single-video tests.\n\nTest 2: with lesson JSON.\n\n```python\ndef test_real_extract_single_video_with_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/chapter/3/lesson/7'  # depends on _VALID_URL\n    webpage = (\n        '<html>'\n        \"player.playlist = [{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8'}];\"\n        \"window.lesson = {'lesson_name': 'My Lesson', 'md_desc': 'Lesson MD desc', 'date': '2019-06-01', 'duration': '10 minutes'};\"\n        '</html>'\n    )\n```\n\nWait \u2014 JSON must use double quotes for strict JSON. `json.dumps` the lesson dict. And playlist JSON too.\n\n```python\nplaylist_json = json.dumps([{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8'}])\nlesson_json = json.dumps({\n    'lesson_name': 'My Lesson',\n    'md_desc': 'Lesson MD desc',\n    'date': '2019-06-01',\n    'duration': '10 minutes',\n})\nwebpage = 'player.playlist = %s; window.lesson = %s;' % (playlist_json, lesson_json)\n```\n\nCareful: the playlist regex `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 non-greedy `\\[.+?\\]` up to first `];`. The JSON `[{\"file\": \"...\"}]` contains `]` only at end. Good. But note `.+?` doesn't match newline