ould handle dicts without those keys. But there's a subtlety: comparing None with int fails in Py3? The implementation handles missing fields by using a default. I think it's safe. But monkeypatching is safer and keeps test hermetic. I'll monkeypatch.\n\nNow, about `_search_regex` \u2014 real one. It needs `self._downloader`? On failure with default set, returns default. On success returns match. It may call `self._downloader.report_warning`? Let me check the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef _search_regex(self, pattern, string, name, default=NO_DEFAULT, fatal=True, flags=0, group=None):\n    ...\n    mobj = re.search(pattern, string, flags)\n    if mobj:\n        if group is None:\n            return next(g for g in mobj.groups() if g is not None)\n        else:\n            return mobj.group(group)\n    elif default is not NO_DEFAULT:\n        return default\n    elif fatal:\n        raise ExtractorError('Unable to extract %s' % _html ... )\n    else:\n        self.report_warning('unable to extract %s' % name)\n```\n\n`report_warning`:\n\n```python\ndef report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n    ...\n    if self._downloader:\n        self._downloader.report_warning(msg)\n    else:\n        self.to_screen ...\n```\n\nHmm. Actually in youtube-dl common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n    '''\n    Report a warning.\n    @param msg      message to display\n    '''\n    msg = msg.strip()\n    if self._downloader is not None:\n        self._downloader.report_warning(msg)\n    else:\n        self.to_stderr(msg)\n```\n\nHmm, not sure about to_stderr. Let me check via get_info. If `_search_regex` with fatal=False and no match calls report_warning which needs downloader... In our tests, `_search_regex` calls always match or have defaults, so no warning path. Actually the lesson search has `default='{}'` \u2014 if no lesson in webpage, returns '{}' without warning. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 in the single-video test without lesson, `_search