tance attribute, leaving pollution! Hmm.\n\nLet me check pytest monkeypatch semantics: `monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value, raising=True)`. The undo: `if name in self._setattr: ...` it stores `(obj, name, value)` and on undo does `setattr(obj, name, oldval)` \u2014 wait no.\n\nLooking at pytest source (_pytest/monkeypatch.py):\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nand undo:\n\n```python\nfor obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n    if value is not notset:\n        setattr(obj, name, value)\n    else:\n        delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance where the attribute doesn't exist in instance `__dict__` but exists on the class: `oldval = getattr(target, name)` \u2192 the bound method (notset? no, it's found via getattr). Then on undo, `setattr(obj, name, bound_method)` \u2014 this sets an instance attribute pointing to the bound method of the original instance... wait, the bound method is bound to `ie` itself. So after undo, `ie._download_webpage` would be an instance attribute = bound method. That's pollution of that instance, but the instance is local to the test and garbage collected. Since each test creates its own IE instance, there's no cross-test pollution. The class attribute is untouched. So it's fine.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `if inspect.isclass(target): oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)`. For instance target, oldval = getattr(target, name, notset) \u2192 bound method (exists). So undo does setattr(instance, name, bound_method). Instance-level pollution only, instance dies with test. OK, acceptable. \n\nAlternatively, use `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', ...)` on the class \u2014 monkeypatch restores properly for classes (uses `target.__dict__`). But patching the class affects all instances \u2014 fine since we create instances within the test. But if the patch is class-level and two tests run... monkeypatch undoes after each test. Class-level patching is cleaner for restoration. But then the fake function receives `self` as first arg when called as `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. So signature: `def fake_download(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs): return webpage`.\n\nI'll do class-level patching via monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake) \u2014 clean restore.\n\nHmm, but careful: `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 since it's a class, oldval = target.__dict__.get(name) \u2192 the original function \u2192 restored via setattr \u2192 back to normal function (unbound). Good, no pollution.\n\nLet me write helper:\n\n```python\ndef _patch_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    downloaded = {}\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded['url'] = url\n        downloaded['video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n\n    def fake_extract_m3u8(self, m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n        return [{\n            'format_id': 'hls-test',\n            'url': m3u8_url,\n            'ext': ext,\n            'protocol': kwargs.get('entry_protocol'),\n            'm3u8_id': kwargs.get('m3u8_id'),\n        }]\n\n    def fake_sort(self, formats=None, field_preference=None):\n        fake_sort.called = True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_sort_formats', fake_sort)\n    return downloaded\n```\n\nHmm, `fake_sort.called` \u2014 function attribute; fine.\n\nNow the course test:\n\n```python\ndef test_real_extract_course(monkeypatch):\n    course_url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/course/dashboard/my-course'  # depends on _VALID_URL\n    module = {...}\n    webpage = '<html>window.module = %s;</html>' % json.dumps(module)\n    ...\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    patch...\n    result = ie._real_extract(course_url)  # or ie.extract(course_url)?\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 should I call `_real_extract` directly or `extract`? `extract` does more (calls `_real_extract` after checks). The lines in question are in `_real_extract`. Calling `ie._real_extract(url)` directly is simplest and avoids `extract`'s extra machinery (like `_match_id`, suitable checks). But `extract` also handles `_type: playlist` via process... Actually `extract` returns the raw result of `_real_extract` (the ie_result), and playlist processing happens later in YoutubeDL. So `extract` would work too. But `extract` calls `self._match_entry`? No... `extract`:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    ...\n    self._match_valid_url? \n    ie_result = self._real_extract(url)\n    ...\n    return ie_result\n```\n\nIt also does `self.report_extraction`? Something like that which may need downloader. To be safe, call `_real_extract` directly. The coverage target is `_real_extract` lines anyway.\n\nHmm, but is calling a private method OK for a test? It's common in these coverage-driven exercises. Yes.\n\nNow, the URL for course path. I need _VALID_URL. Let me guess from the real youtube-dl source. I'm fairly confident the real file is:\n\n```python\nclass LinuxAcademyIE(InfoExtractor):\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n        https?://\n            (?:\n                linuxacademy\\.com/|\n                (?:www\\.)?app\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n            )\n        (?:\n            course/dashboard/(?P<course_id>[^/?#&]+)|\n            chapter/(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#&]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#&]+)\n        )\n    '''\n```\n\nHmm wait, but then `chapter_id` group would capture e.g. '1' and lesson_id '2'. And item_id = '1-2'.\n\nActually, I found it \u2014 the real youtube-dl linuxacademy.py:\n\n```python\n# coding: utf-8\nfrom __future__ import unicode_literals\n\nimport re\n\nfrom .common import InfoExtractor\nfrom ..compat import compat_str\nfrom ..utils import (\n    clean_html,\n    parse_duration,\n    try_get,\n    unified_timestamp,\n    urljoin,\n)\n\n\nclass LinuxAcademyIE(InfoExtractor):\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n        https?://\n            (?:\n                linuxacademy\\.com/|\n                (?:www\\.)?app\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n            )\n        (?:\n            course/dashboard/(?P<course_id>[^/?#&]+)|\n            chapter/(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#&]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#&]+)\n        )\n    '''\n    _TESTS = [{\n        'url': 'https://linuxacademy.com/course/dashboard-course/elastic-stack-deep-dive/',\n        ...\n```\n\nI really can't recall precisely. MUST use get_info. Let me do that.\n\nAlso get_info for 'InfoExtractor._sort_formats' to decide whether to patch.\n\nLet me also consider: does the module have `LinuxAcademyIE` importable without network? Yes.\n\nNow let me also double-check `unified_timestamp` behavior for '2019-01-02' \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more consideration: `type_field` uses `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)`. For items where 'type' is a dict with 'name'/'slug'. Good.\n\nEdge: For the non-dict item 'not-a-dict' \u2014 `isinstance(item, dict)` False \u2192 continue. Good.\n\nNow, what about `module['items']` \u2014 module is parsed JSON; items list. Good.\n\nNow the playlist result assertions:\n\n```python\nassert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\nassert result['id'] == course_id\nassert result['title'] == 'Test Course'\nassert result['description'] == 'Course description'\nassert result['duration'] == 3600\nentries = result['entries']\nassert len(entries) == 3\n```\n\nEntry A:\n```python\na = entries[0]\nassert a['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\nassert a['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\nassert a['url'] == urljoin(course_url, '/lesson/a')\nassert a['title'] == 'Lesson A'\nassert a['description'] == 'MD desc A'\nassert a['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')\nassert a['duration'] == 300\nassert a['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\nassert a['chapter_id'] == 'module-1'\nassert a['chapter_number'] == 1\n```\n\nEntry B:\n```python\nb = entries[1]\nassert b['url'] == 'https://example.com/lesson/b'\nassert b['title'] == 'Lesson B'\nassert b['description'] == 'Desc B'\nassert b['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-02')\nassert b['duration'] == 360\nassert b['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\nassert b['chapter_id'] == 'module-2'\nassert b['chapter_number'] == 2\n```\n\nEntry C:\n```python\nc = entries[2]\nassert c['title'] == 'Lesson C'\nassert c['description'] == 'Text C'\nassert c['timestamp'] is None  # no date/created_on\nassert c['duration'] is None\nassert c['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\nassert c['chapter_number'] == 2\n```\n\nWait \u2014 entry C has no date/created_on \u2192 `unified_timestamp(None)` \u2192 None? unified_timestamp(None) \u2014 does it handle None? `unified_timestamp(date_str)`: \n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n    ...\n```\n\nI believe it returns None for None. Yes, it starts with `if date_str is None: return None`. Good.\n\nparse_duration(None) \u2192 None. Good.\n\nAlso, `title = item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` \u2014 for entry C, title present. For entry A, lesson_name. Covered.\n\nHmm wait, for entry C I set 'title': 'Lesson C' and text description. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing about `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')`: I want to make sure it doesn't raise. It should parse ISO date. Actually, let me double check youtube-dl's unified_timestamp with '2019-01-01':\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    ...\n    for regex in ...:\n    ...\n```\n\nIt has special handling: `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$`? There's `_DATE_FORMATS`... I'm fairly confident ISO dates work. To be extra safe, use a fuller format like '2019-01-01 12:00:00' or 'January 1, 2019'. Hmm. unified_timestamp handles many formats including 'YYYY-MM-DD'. I'll trust it but compute expected via unified_timestamp in the test so the assertion is robust even if the exact epoch differs.\n\nActually, hmm, there's a subtlety: unified_timestamp('2019-01-01') might interpret with timezone UTC \u2192 1546300800. If it used local time it'd vary, but youtube-dl parses dates as UTC by default (uses `datetime` with timezone.utc or calendar.timegm). Comparing to `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` in the test avoids any issue.\n\nFor created_on '2019-01-02' similarly.\n\nNow the single-video tests.\n\nTest 2: with lesson JSON.\n\n```python\ndef test_real_extract_single_video_with_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/chapter/3/lesson/7'  # depends on _VALID_URL\n    webpage = (\n        '<html>'\n        \"player.playlist = [{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8'}];\"\n        \"window.lesson = {'lesson_name': 'My Lesson', 'md_desc': 'Lesson MD desc', 'date': '2019-06-01', 'duration': '10 minutes'};\"\n        '</html>'\n    )\n```\n\nWait \u2014 JSON must use double quotes for strict JSON. `json.dumps` the lesson dict. And playlist JSON too.\n\n```python\nplaylist_json = json.dumps([{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8'}])\nlesson_json = json.dumps({\n    'lesson_name': 'My Lesson',\n    'md_desc': 'Lesson MD desc',\n    'date': '2019-06-01',\n    'duration': '10 minutes',\n})\nwebpage = 'player.playlist = %s; window.lesson = %s;' % (playlist_json, lesson_json)\n```\n\nCareful: the playlist regex `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 non-greedy `\\[.+?\\]` up to first `];`. The JSON `[{\"file\": \"...\"}]` contains `]` only at end. Good. But note `.+?` doesn't match newline \u2014 keep on one line. Good.\n\nThe lesson regex: `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 `{.+?}` non-greedy to first `}` followed by `\\s*;`. JSON `{\"lesson_name\": \"My Lesson\", ...}` \u2014 inner objects? None (flat dict). The first `}` is the closing brace. Good. But careful: if the JSON contains nested objects, non-greedy would stop early. Flat dict is fine.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\nresult = ie._real_extract(url)\nassert result['id'] == '3-7'\nassert result['formats'] == [{'format_id': 'hls-test', 'url': 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'}]\nassert result['title'] == 'My Lesson'\nassert result['description'] == 'Lesson MD desc'\nassert result['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-01')\nassert result['duration'] == 600\n```\n\nparse_duration('10 minutes') = 600. Good.\n\nAlso assert `_sort_formats` was called (fake_sort.called). And that `_download_webpage` got the right url/video_id: downloaded['url'] == url, downloaded['video_id'] == '3-7'.\n\nTest 3: no lesson \u2192 title fallback via '>Lecture:'.\n\n```python\nwebpage = \"player.playlist = [{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/x.m3u8'}]; <h1>Lecture: Fallback Title</h1>\"\n```\n\nWait, the regex `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` \u2014 needs `>Lecture:` literally. So webpage must contain `>Lecture: Fallback Title`. E.g. `<div>Lecture: Fallback Title</div>` \u2014 the `>` before Lecture: `<div>Lecture:` \u2192 `>Lecture: Fallback Title` matches with value ' Fallback Title'? `\\s*` after colon consumes leading spaces; `[^<]+` captures 'Fallback Title'. Good.\n\nThen result['title'] == 'Fallback Title'. And lesson absent \u2192 no title/description/timestamp/duration from lesson. Assert 'title' == 'Fallback Title', and description/timestamp/duration not in result (info only has id and formats plus title).\n\nTest 4: no lesson, title via `lessonName = 'Another Title'`:\n\n```python\nwebpage = \"player.playlist = [...]; var lessonName = 'Another Title';\"\n```\n\nRegex: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 matches `lessonName = 'Another Title'` \u2192 value 'Another Title'. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: in test 3 and 4, `_search_regex` for lesson returns default '{}' \u2192 `_parse_json('{}', ...)` \u2192 {} \u2192 falsy. Good.\n\nAlso, should I test `_parse_json` failure path (invalid lesson JSON with fatal=False \u2192 None)? That covers `fatal=False` behavior but it's in common.py, not our lines. The `if lesson:` False branch is covered by tests 3/4. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 do I need to worry about `mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')`? `match.group(*names)` with multiple names returns tuple. Fine.\n\nNow, the exact _VALID_URL \u2014 let me fetch. Also fetch InfoExtractor methods to confirm signatures for the fakes.\n\nAlso \u2014 one more consideration: `ie._real_extract(url)` calls `re.match(self._VALID_URL, url)`. If URL doesn't match, mobj is None \u2192 AttributeError. So URLs must match. Get _VALID_URL first.\n\nLet me also think about whether `LinuxAcademyIE` has `_WORKING` or geo-bypass stuff that runs in `_real_extract` \u2014 no.\n\nPotential issue: `InfoExtractor.__init__` might require downloader for `_download_webpage` \u2014 patched, fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: `_parse_json` in newer youtube-dl versions calls `transform_source` and also `self._search_regex`? No.\n\nOK, let me also double check `clean_html` behavior: `clean_html('<b>Course description</b>')` \u2192 'Course description'. Yes.\n\n`clean_html('<i>Desc B</i>')` \u2192 'Desc B'. `clean_html('<p>Text C</p>')` \u2192 'Text C'. Good.\n\nNow about `urljoin(url, item.get('url'))` where url is the course page URL. If course URL ends with '/course/dashboard/my-course', urljoin(base, '/lesson/a') \u2192 scheme+host + '/lesson/a'. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 what if _VALID_URL's course_id group is something like `course/dashboard/(?P<course_id>[^/?#&]+)`? Then URL 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/course/dashboard/my-course' matches with course_id='my-course'. \n\nAlternatively the real _VALID_URL might be:\n\n```python\n_VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n    https?://\n        (?:\n            linuxacademy\\.com/|\n            (?:www\\.)?app\\.linuxacademy\\.com/\n        )\n    (?:\n        course/dashboard/(?P<course_id>[^/?#&]+)|\n        chapter/(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#&]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#&]+)\n    )\n'''\n```\n\nLet me just call get_info.\n\nActually, I realize I should also check `InfoExtractor.ie_key` to assert correctly. `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2192 'LinuxAcademy'. I'll assert `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 self-consistent.\n\nLet me make the get_info calls now:\n\n1. `LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL`\n2. `InfoExtractor._search_regex`\n3. `InfoExtractor._parse_json`\n4. `InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats`\n5. `InfoExtractor._sort_formats`\n6. `InfoExtractor._download_webpage`\n7. `unified_timestamp`\n8. `parse_duration`\n9. `urljoin`\n10. `clean_html`\n11. `InfoExtractor.ie_key`\n\nThese are all independent \u2014 batch them.\n\nThen write the final test file.\n\nLet me draft the full test file now (to be refined after get_info):\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import parse_duration, unified_timestamp, urljoin\n\n\ndef _install_fake_io(monkeypatch, webpage):\n    \"\"\"Patch network-facing helpers of LinuxAcademyIE with fakes.\"\"\"\n    calls = {'download': [], 'm3u8': [], 'sorted': False}\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        calls['download'].append((url, video_id))\n        return webpage\n\n    def fake_extract_m3u8_formats(self, m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n        calls['m3u8'].append((m3u8_url, video_id, ext, kwargs))\n        return [{\n            'format_id': '%s-test' % kwargs.get('m3u8_id'),\n            'url': m3u8_url,\n            'ext': ext,\n            'protocol': kwargs.get('entry_protocol'),\n        }]\n\n    def fake_sort_formats(self, formats=None, field_preference=None):\n        calls['sorted'] = True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8_formats)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_sort_formats', fake_sort_formats)\n    return calls\n\n\ndef test_course_playlist(monkeypatch):\n    course_id = 'linux-academy-weekly-news'\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/course/dashboard/%s' % course_id\n    module = {\n        'title': 'Test Course',\n        'md_desc': '<b>Course description</b>',\n        'duration': '1 hour',\n        'items': [\n            'not-a-dict',\n            {'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n             'course_name': 'Chapter One', 'course_module': 'module-1'},\n            {'type': {'name': 'Quiz', 'slug': 'quiz'}},\n            {'type': {'name': 'Lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n             'lesson_name': 'Lesson A', 'url': '/lesson/a',\n             'date': '2019-01-01', 'duration': '5 minutes',\n             'md_desc': 'MD desc A'},\n            {'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n             'course_name': 'Chapter Two', 'course_module': 'module-2'},\n            {'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n             'title': 'Lesson B', 'url': 'https://example.com/lesson/b',\n             'created_on': '2019-01-02', 'duration': '6:00',\n             'description': '<i>Desc B</i>'},\n            {'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n             'title': 'Lesson C', 'url': '/lesson/c',\n             'text': '<p>Text C</p>'},\n            {'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n             'title': 'Lesson D'},  # no url -> skipped\n        ],\n    }\n    webpage = '<html><script>window.module = %s;</script></html>' % json.dumps(module)\n    calls = _install_fake_io(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    # webpage was fetched with the expected args\n    assert calls['download'] == [(url, course_id)]\n\n    assert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\n    assert result['id'] == course_id\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Course'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Course description'\n    assert result['duration'] == 3600\n\n    entries = result['entries']\n    assert len(entries) == 3  # non-dict, quiz and url-less lesson are skipped\n\n    a, b, c = entries\n    assert a['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\n    assert a['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\n    assert a['url'] == urljoin(url, '/lesson/a')\n    assert a['title'] == 'Lesson A'\n    assert a['description'] == 'MD desc A'\n    assert a['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')\n    assert a['duration'] == 300\n    assert a['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\n    assert a['chapter_id'] == 'module-1'\n    assert a['chapter_number'] == 1\n\n    assert b['url'] == 'https://example.com/lesson/b'\n    assert b['title'] == 'Lesson B'\n    assert b['description'] == 'Desc B'\n    assert b['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-02')\n    assert b['duration'] == 360\n    assert b['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\n    assert b['chapter_id'] == 'module-2'\n    assert b['chapter_number'] == 2\n\n    assert c['url'] == urljoin(url, '/lesson/c')\n    assert c['title'] == 'Lesson C'\n    assert c['description'] == 'Text C'\n    assert c['timestamp'] is None\n    assert c['duration'] is None\n    assert c['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\n    assert c['chapter_number'] == 2\n```\n\nWait \u2014 entry D has no url \u2192 skipped. But hold on: entry D has 'title' but no url \u2192 `lesson_url = urljoin(url, None)` \u2192 None \u2192 continue. Good, covers line 188-189.\n\nHmm, but wait: does `urljoin(base, None)` return None? youtube-dl's urljoin:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(base, bytes):\n        ...\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str):\n        return None\n    if not isinstance(base, compat_str):\n        return None\n    return urllib.parse.urljoin(base, path)\n```\n\nSomething like that. Yes, returns None for non-str path. Good.\n\nNow single video tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_with_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/chapter/3/lesson/7'\n    m3u8_url = 'https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8'\n    playlist = json.dumps([{'file': m3u8_url}])\n    lesson = json.dumps({\n        'lesson_name': 'My Lesson',\n        'md_desc': 'Lesson MD desc',\n        'date': '2019-06-01',\n        'duration': '10 minutes',\n    })\n    webpage = 'player.playlist = %s; window.lesson = %s;' % (playlist, lesson)\n    calls = _install_fake_io(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    assert calls['download'] == [(url, '3-7')]\n    assert calls['m3u8'] == [(m3u8_url, '3-7', 'mp4', {'entry_protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'})]\n    assert calls['sorted'] is True\n\n    assert result['id'] == '3-7'\n    assert result['formats'] == [{'format_id': 'hls-test', 'url': m3u8_url, 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native'}]\n    assert result['title'] == 'My Lesson'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Lesson MD desc'\n    assert result['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-01')\n    assert result['duration'] == 600\n```\n\nHmm, careful with the m3u8 call kwargs assertion \u2014 `_real_extract` calls:\n\n```python\nself._extract_m3u8_formats(\n    m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4', entry_protocol='m3u8_native',\n    m3u8_id='hls')\n```\n\nSo kwargs = {'entry_protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'}. My fake signature: `def fake_extract_m3u8_formats(self, m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs)`. Called with positional (m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4') and kwargs. Good.\n\nBut asserting exact kwargs dict equality is brittle if the real code passes more kwargs \u2014 it doesn't. OK.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: just record m3u8_url and assert formats content. I'll keep the call record but assert loosely:\n\n```python\nassert len(calls['m3u8']) == 1\nm3u8_call = calls['m3u8'][0]\nassert m3u8_call[0] == m3u8_url\nassert m3u8_call[1] == '3-7'\nassert m3u8_call[2] == 'mp4'\nassert m3u8_call[3]['entry_protocol'] == 'm3u8_native'\nassert m3u8_call[3]['m3u8_id'] == 'hls'\n```\n\nTest 3: fallback title via Lecture regex:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_title_fallback_lecture(monkeypatch):\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/chapter/1/lesson/2'\n    webpage = (\n        \"player.playlist = \" + json.dumps([{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/a.m3u8'}]) + \";\"\n        \"<h1>Lecture: Fallback Title</h1>\"\n    )\n    calls = _install_fake_io(monkeypatch, webpage)\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n    assert result['id'] == '1-2'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Fallback Title'\n    assert 'description' not in result\n    assert 'timestamp' not in result\n    assert 'duration' not in result\n    assert len(result['formats']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the playlist regex: `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;`. The webpage: `player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/a.m3u8\"}];<h1>Lecture: Fallback Title</h1>`. The regex `\\[.+?\\]` non-greedy: matches `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/a.m3u8\"}]` \u2014 first `]` is at the end of the array. Good. Then `\\s*;` matches `;`. Good.\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: `.+?` \u2014 the JSON contains `\"` and `/` \u2014 fine. But does it contain `]` earlier? The URL has no `]`. Good.\n\nTest 4: fallback via lessonName regex:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_title_fallback_lesson_name(monkeypatch):\n    url = 'https://app.linuxacademy.com/chapter/4/lesson/9'\n    webpage = (\n        \"player.playlist = \" + json.dumps([{'file': 'https://example.com/hls/b.m3u8'}]) + \";\"\n        \"var lessonName = 'Another Fallback';\"\n    )\n    ...\n    assert result['title'] == 'Another Fallback'\n```\n\nRegex: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 matches `lessonName = 'Another Fallback'`. group('value') = 'Another Fallback'. Good.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, in test 4, `_search_regex` for lesson: patterns `window\\.lesson...` and `player\\.lesson...` \u2014 neither present \u2192 default '{}' \u2192 OK.\n\nBut careful: in test 4, the webpage contains `lessonName` \u2014 does the lesson regex `player\\.lesson\\s*=` match? No, it's `lessonName`, not `player.lesson`. Good.\n\nAlso in test 3, webpage contains `Lecture:` \u2014 no interference.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing to double check: in test 3 and 4, `if lesson:` \u2014 lesson = {} \u2192 falsy \u2192 skip. Then `if not info.get('title'):` \u2192 True \u2192 search regex. Good.\n\nNow, about `_parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False)` \u2014 returns {}. Good.\n\nNow, what about the `chapter_id`/`lesson_id` group names in _VALID_URL \u2014 the code does `mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')`. So the regex must have those named groups. My URLs must match accordingly.\n\nNow let me also think: is there any issue with `_real_extract` being called directly vs through `extract`? `extract` would call `self._match_id`? No \u2014 `extract` does:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    ...\n    self._downloader ... report stuff?\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    \"\"\"Extract url information\"\"\"\n    ...\n    self._url = url ... \n    ie_result = self._real_extract(url)\n    ...\n    if self._downloader:\n        ...\n    return ie_result\n```\n\nThere's `self.to_screen(...)` calls guarded by `if self._downloader`. Calling `_real_extract` directly avoids all that. I'll call `_real_extract` directly.\n\nNow, one more consideration \u2014 the `get_info` tool: \"Returns information about a symbol.\" I'll query the needed symbols. Let me batch:\n\n- LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL\n- InfoExtractor._download_webpage\n- InfoExtractor._search_regex\n- InfoExtractor._parse_json\n- InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats\n- InfoExtractor._sort_formats\n- InfoExtractor.ie_key\n- unified_timestamp\n- parse_duration\n- urljoin\n- clean_html\n\nThat's a lot of calls; maybe trim to the essential: _VALID_URL (critical), _extract_m3u8_formats signature, _sort_formats, _search_regex, _parse_json, unified_timestamp, parse_duration, urljoin. I'll do them all in one batch since independent.\n\nAfter getting info, finalize the test code.\n\nOne more thought on coverage: lines 155-243 include everything in `_real_extract`. My tests cover:\n\n- 155-159: both paths\n- 162-166: course path entry\n- 167-170: init\n- 171-173: loop + non-dict skip\n- 175-177: type_field\n- 179-183: section branch (both chapter_number branches: 1 and +1)\n- 185-186: non-lesson skip\n- 187-189: lesson_url None skip\n- 190-202: entry building (title both ways, description all three ways, timestamp both ways, duration)\n- 204-210: playlist return\n- 214-221: single video m3u8 + formats + sort\n- 222-225: info dict\n- 226-237: lesson present branch\n- 238-243: title fallback (both regexes via two tests)\n\nBranch coverage: `if course_id` both True/False; `if not isinstance(item, dict)` both; `'section' in type_fields` both; `'lesson' not in type_fields` both; `if not lesson_url` both; `1 if not chapter_number else +1` both; `item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` both; description chain all; timestamp chain both; `if lesson` both; `if not info.get('title')` both.\n\nGreat.\n\nNow, potential gotcha: `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` \u2014 let me make sure it doesn't return None. youtube-dl's unified_timestamp:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    ...\n    # TODO: ...\n    for expr in (...):\n        ...\n```\n\nIt handles ISO 8601 dates like '2015-05-05'. I'm confident. But to be extra safe, I could use a more explicit format like '2019-01-01T00:00:00' or 'Jan 1, 2019'. Hmm, unified_timestamp with '2019-01-01' \u2014 there's a regex `(?P<date>\\d{4}-\\d{1,2}-\\d{1,2})` handled via `datetime.datetime.strptime(date, '%Y-%m-%d')`... I believe it works. I'll still compare against `unified_timestamp(...)` computed in the test, so even if it returns some value, the assertion checks pass-through. But if it returned None, the assertion `a['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` would still pass (None == None) \u2014 which would weaken the test. To ensure it's meaningful, maybe assert `a['timestamp'] is not None` too. Let me add that.\n\nActually, let me verify with get_info on unified_timestamp to see implementation notes. The get_info tool returns \"information about a symbol\" \u2014 probably source code. That'll let me confirm.\n\nSimilarly parse_duration('5 minutes') \u2192 300, parse_duration('6:00') \u2192 360, parse_duration('1 hour') \u2192 3600, parse_duration('10 minutes') \u2192 600. I'm confident about these.\n\nLet me also double check `clean_html` \u2014 it's imported in the module from ..utils. In my test I can import from youtube_dl.utils too.\n\nNow about the `pytest` import \u2014 I use `monkeypatch` fixture; need `import pytest`? Only if using pytest.raises or marks. I don't strictly need pytest import if I only use fixtures via test args. But it's fine to import pytest (flake might complain unused). I'll avoid importing pytest if unused. Actually I might not need it. Let me not import it unless needed.\n\nTop-level code: just imports and helper functions + test functions. No pytest.main. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the fake `_download_webpage` signature \u2014 the real call is `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. Since I patch the class attribute with a plain function, `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)` \u2192 `fake(self, url, item_id)`. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is `_download_webpage` maybe wrapped by a decorator in the class (like `@staticmethod`)? No, it's a regular method.\n\nAlso `_extract_m3u8_formats` \u2014 regular method.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` \u2014 real. It might use `self._downloader`? No.\n\n`_search_regex` \u2014 real. On success no downloader needed.\n\nEdge: In the course test, `_search_regex(r'window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;', webpage, 'module')` \u2014 my webpage has `window.module = {...};` on one line inside `<script>` tags. The regex will find it. Good.\n\nBut careful \u2014 `json.dumps(module)` produces a single-line JSON (no indent). Good. It contains `{` and `}` \u2014 the regex `({.+?})` non-greedy: starts at first `{` after `window.module = `, matches minimally up to first `}` followed by `\\s*;`. The JSON's first `}` \u2014 is it followed by `;`? The JSON has nested objects: `{'type': {...}, ...}` \u2014 the inner `}` of type objects are followed by `,` not `;`. The final `}` of the module object is followed by `;`. Non-greedy `.+?` will try to match as little as possible such that the rest matches `\\s*;`. The regex engine will find the first `}` position where the remainder matches `\\s*;` \u2014 i.e., the LAST `}` before `;`... wait no.\n\nLet me think: pattern `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`. After matching `window.module = `, it tries `{`, then `.+?` minimal, then `}`, then `\\s*;`. The engine expands `.+?` until the whole pattern matches. The first successful match: `.+?` grows character by character; at each step checks if `}` matches current char and then `\\s*;` matches the rest. The first `}` in the string that is followed by optional whitespace and `;` \u2014 hmm, but `.+?` could also stop earlier if the next char is `}` and then `\\s*;` matches. The inner `}` chars are followed by `,` \u2192 `\\s*;` fails \u2192 continue expanding. Eventually reaches the final `}` followed by `;` \u2192 match. So the full JSON object is captured. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a catch: the JSON contains `}` inside... e.g. `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"Section\"}}` \u2014 the inner `}` followed by `}` then `}`... The first `}` (closing 'type') is followed by `}` (closing item) \u2014 `\\s*;` fails. Then next `}` followed by `,` \u2014 fails. Etc. Only the last `}` before `;` succeeds. But hold on \u2014 what about `}` followed by whitespace then `;`? Only at the very end. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtle issue: `.+?` is non-greedy but the overall match must succeed; the engine finds the LEFTMOST match overall \u2014 since `window.module` appears once, fine.\n\nSame for lesson JSON (flat) and playlist JSON (array with one object: `[{\"file\": \"...\"}]` \u2014 the `]` appears only at end; `.+?` expands until `]` followed by `;`. The `]` inside? None. Good.)\n\nWait, in the playlist JSON `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8\"}]` \u2014 any `]` inside? No. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `_parse_json` on the captured playlist string: `json.loads('[{\"file\": \"...\"}]')` \u2192 list. `[0]['file']` \u2192 m3u8_url. Good.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` in youtube-dl might have `ignore_extra` or handle trailing stuff \u2014 fine.\n\nAnother consideration: `_parse_json` may call `transform_source=None` \u2192 no transform. It uses `json.loads` with `object_pairs_hook`? In newer versions there's handling for `undefined` via `_JSON_OPERATORS`... For strict JSON input, fine.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `ie._real_extract(url)` for the course path needs `self._downloader` anywhere \u2014 `_parse_json` no, `_search_regex` no (match succeeds). Good.\n\nWhat about `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 classmethod, no instance needed. Good.\n\nNow, let me also handle the possibility that `_VALID_URL` requires specific URL shapes. I'll fetch it.\n\nLet me also double-check `mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')` \u2014 for the course URL, chapter_id and lesson_id are None; course_id is the captured group. For single video URL, course_id None.\n\nOK \u2014 let me make the get_info calls now.\n\nActually, one more thing: the test file should be self-contained and runnable. The module path: `youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy`. The snippet shows the class defined with those imports \u2014 consistent with the real module.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I worry that `LinuxAcademyIE` might not be exported? Direct module import works.\n\nLet me now write the get_info calls.\n\nAfter that, finalize.\n\nOne more design decision: instead of patching class methods with monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, ...), I could create the instance and patch instance attributes. Class-level patching with monkeypatch restores cleanly (uses __dict__). I'll go class-level.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety with class-level patching and `monkeypatch.setattr`: for classes, `oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)`; the original `_download_webpage` is defined in `InfoExtractor` (parent), not in `LinuxAcademyIE.__dict__`! So `LinuxAcademyIE.__dict__.get('_download_webpage')` \u2192 notset \u2192 with raising=True \u2192 AttributeError!\n\nHmm! That's a problem. `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake, raising=True)` would raise AttributeError because LinuxAcademyIE doesn't define `_download_webpage` in its own `__dict__` (it inherits from InfoExtractor).\n\nWait, let me re-check pytest's monkeypatch.setattr:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(\"%r object has no attribute %r\" % (target, name))\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nSo `oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)` \u2014 for the class, getattr finds the inherited method \u2192 not notset \u2192 no raise. Then since target is a class, oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset) \u2192 notset (since it's inherited). Then undo: `if value is not notset: setattr(obj, name, value) else: delattr(obj, name)` \u2014 value is notset \u2192 `delattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage')` \u2192 deletes the class attribute that monkeypatch itself set \u2192 restores inheritance. \n\nSo class-level patching works correctly: it sets the attribute on LinuxAcademyIE (shadowing the inherited one) and on undo deletes it, restoring the inherited method. No pollution. \n\nAlternatively, instance-level: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 oldval = getattr(ie, ...) \u2192 bound method (exists) \u2192 stored; undo: setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', bound_method) \u2192 leaves instance attribute pointing to bound method of that instance. Instance is discarded \u2192 no cross-test pollution. Also fine.\n\nI'll use class-level patching \u2014 cleaner semantics.\n\nHmm, but one concern with class-level patching: if tests run in parallel or the class is used elsewhere during the test \u2014 not an issue here.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether to patch `_sort_formats` or use the real one. Let me get_info on it to decide. If the real one works with simple dicts, using it adds a bit more realism. But its behavior with my fake format dicts: it sorts by preference fields; missing fields \u2192 treated as... Let me check the implementation via get_info.\n\nActually, you know, let me keep the fake to keep the test hermetic and focused. But I want to assert `_sort_formats` was called \u2014 my fake records that.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe better to let the real `_sort_formats` run to avoid patching more than needed? The risk: `_sort_formats` might raise on formats lacking required keys. Let me check its source via get_info and decide. If it's safe, I'll use the real one with well-formed fake formats (each with 'format_id', 'url', 'ext', 'preference'...). Actually the real `_sort_formats` in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats=None, field_preference=None):\n    if not