ex: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 matches `lessonName = 'Another Fallback'`. group('value') = 'Another Fallback'. Good.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, in test 4, `_search_regex` for lesson: patterns `window\\.lesson...` and `player\\.lesson...` \u2014 neither present \u2192 default '{}' \u2192 OK.\n\nBut careful: in test 4, the webpage contains `lessonName` \u2014 does the lesson regex `player\\.lesson\\s*=` match? No, it's `lessonName`, not `player.lesson`. Good.\n\nAlso in test 3, webpage contains `Lecture:` \u2014 no interference.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing to double check: in test 3 and 4, `if lesson:` \u2014 lesson = {} \u2192 falsy \u2192 skip. Then `if not info.get('title'):` \u2192 True \u2192 search regex. Good.\n\nNow, about `_parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False)` \u2014 returns {}. Good.\n\nNow, what about the `chapter_id`/`lesson_id` group names in _VALID_URL \u2014 the code does `mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')`. So the regex must have those named groups. My URLs must match accordingly.\n\nNow let me also think: is there any issue with `_real_extract` being called directly vs through `extract`? `extract` would call `self._match_id`? No \u2014 `extract` does:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    ...\n    self._downloader ... report stuff?\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef extract(self, url):\n    \"\"\"Extract url information\"\"\"\n    ...\n    self._url = url ... \n    ie_result = self._real_extract(url)\n    ...\n    if self._downloader:\n        ...\n    return ie_result\n```\n\nThere's `self.to_screen(...)` calls guarded by `if self._downloader`. Calling `_real_extract` directly avoids all that. I'll call `_real_extract` directly.\n\nNow, one more consideration \u2014 the `get_info` tool: \"Returns information about a symbol.\" I'll query the needed symbols. Let me batch:\n\n- LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL\n- InfoExtractor._download_webpage\n- InfoExtractor._search_regex\n- InfoExtractor._parse_json\n- InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats\n- InfoExtractor._sort_formats\n- InfoExtractor.ie_key\n- unified_timestamp\n- parse_duration\n- urljoin\n- clean_html\n\nThat's a lot of calls; maybe trim to the essential: _VALID_URL (critical), _extract_m3u8_formats signature, _sort_formats, _search_regex, _parse_json, unified_timestamp, parse_duration, urljoin. I'll do them all in one batch since independent.\n\nAfter getting info, finalize the test code.\n\nOne more thought on coverage: lines 155-243 include everything in `_real_extract`. My tests cover:\n\n- 155-159: both paths\n- 162-166: course path entry\n- 167-170: init\n- 171-173: loop + non-dict skip\n- 175-177: type_field\n- 179-183: section branch (both chapter_number branches: 1 and +1)\n- 185-186: non-lesson skip\n- 187-189: lesson_url None skip\n- 190-202: entry building (title both ways, description all three ways, timestamp both ways, duration)\n- 204-210: playlist return\n- 214-221: single video m3u8 + formats + sort\n- 222-225: info dict\n- 226-237: lesson present branch\n- 238-243: title fallback (both regexes via two tests)\n\nBranch coverage: `if course_id` both True/False; `if not isinstance(item, dict)` both; `'section' in type_fields` both; `'lesson' not in type_fields` both; `if not lesson_url` both; `1 if not chapter_number else +1` both; `item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` both; description chain all; timestamp chain both; `if lesson` both; `if not info.get('title')` both.\n\nGreat.\n\nNow, potential gotcha: `unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` \u2014 let me make sure it doesn't return None. youtube-dl's unified_timestamp:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    ...\n    # TODO: ...\n    for expr in (...):\n        ...\n```\n\nIt handles ISO 8601 dates like '2015-05-05'. I'm confident. But to be extra safe, I could use a more explicit format like '2019-01-01T00:00:00' or 'Jan 1, 2019'. Hmm, unified_timestamp with '2019-01-01' \u2014 there's a regex `(?P<date>\\d{4}-\\d{1,2}-\\d{1,2})` handled via `datetime.datetime.strptime(date, '%Y-%m-%d')`... I believe it works. I'll still compare against `unified_timestamp(...)` computed in the test, so even if it returns some value, the assertion checks pass-through. But if it returned None, the assertion `a['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-01-01')` would still pass (None == None) \u2014 which would weaken the test. To ensure it's meaningful, maybe assert `a['timestamp'] is not None` too. Let me add that.\n\nActually, let me verify with get_info on unified_timestamp to see implementation notes. The get_info tool returns \"information about a symbol\" \u2014 probably source code. That'll let me confirm.\n\nSimilarly parse_duration('5 minutes') \u2192 300, parse_duration('6:00') \u2192 360, parse_duration('1 hour') \u2192 3600, parse_duration('10 minutes') \u2192 600. I'm confident about these.\n\nLet me also double check `clean_html` \u2014 it's imported in the module from ..utils. In my test I can import from youtube_dl.utils too.\n\nNow about the `pytest` import \u2014 I use `monkeypatch` fixture; need `import pytest`? Only if using pytest.raises or marks. I don't strictly need pytest import if I only use fixtures via test args. But it's fine to import pytest (flake might complain unused). I'll avoid importing pytest if unused. Actually I might not need it. Let me not import it unless needed.\n\nTop-level code: just imports and helper functions + test functions. No pytest.main. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the fake `_download_webpage` signature \u2014 the real call is `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. Since I patch the class attribute with a plain function, `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)` \u2192 `fake(self, url, item_id)`. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is `_download_webpage` maybe wrapped by a decorator in the class (like `@staticmethod`)? No, it's a regular method.\n\nAlso `_extract_m3u8_formats` \u2014 regular method.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` \u2014 real. It might use `self._downloader`? No.\n\n`_search_regex` \u2014 real. On success no downloader needed.\n\nEdge: In the course test, `_search_regex(r'window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;', webpage, 'module')` \u2014 my webpage has `window.module = {...};` on one line inside `<script>` tags. The regex will find it. Good.\n\nBut careful \u2014 `json.dumps(module)` produces a single-line JSON (no indent). Good. It contains `{` and `}` \u2014 the regex `({.+?})` non-greedy: starts at first `{` after `window.module = `, matches minimally up to first `}` followed by `\\s*;`. The JSON's first `}` \u2014 is it followed by `;`? The JSON has nested objects: `{'type': {...}, ...}` \u2014 the inner `}` of type objects are followed by `,` not `;`. The final `}` of the module object is followed by `;`. Non-greedy `.+?` will try to match as little as possible such that the rest matches `\\s*;`. The regex engine will find the first `}` position where the remainder matches `\\s*;` \u2014 i.e., the LAST `}` before `;`... wait no.\n\nLet me think: pattern `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`. After matching `window.module = `, it tries `{`, then `.+?` minimal, then `}`, then `\\s*;`. The engine expands `.+?` until the whole pattern matches. The first successful match: `.+?` grows character by character; at each step checks if `}` matches current char and then `\\s*;` matches the rest. The first `}` in the string that is followed by optional whitespace and `;` \u2014 hmm, but `.+?` could also stop earlier if the next char is `}` and then `\\s*;` matches. The inner `}` chars are followed by `,` \u2192 `\\s*;` fails \u2192 continue expanding. Eventually reaches the final `}` followed by `;` \u2192 match. So the full JSON object is captured. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a catch: the JSON contains `}` inside... e.g. `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"Section\"}}` \u2014 the inner `}` followed by `}` then `}`... The first `}` (closing 'type') is followed by `}` (closing item) \u2014 `\\s*;` fails. Then next `}` followed by `,` \u2014 fails. Etc. Only the last `}` before `;` succeeds. But hold on \u2014 what about `}` followed by whitespace then `;`? Only at the very end. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtle issue: `.+?` is non-greedy but the overall match must succeed; the engine finds the LEFTMOST match overall \u2014 since `window.module` appears once, fine.\n\nSame for lesson JSON (flat) and playlist JSON (array with one object: `[{\"file\": \"...\"}]` \u2014 the `]` appears only at end; `.+?` expands until `]` followed by `;`. The `]` inside? None. Good.)\n\nWait, in the playlist JSON `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/lesson7.m3u8\"}]` \u2014 any `]` inside? No. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `_parse_json` on the captured playlist string: `json.loads('[{\"file\": \"...\"}]')` \u2192 list. `[0]['file']` \u2192 m3u8_url. Good.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` in youtube-dl might have `ignore_extra` or handle trailing stuff \u2014 fine.\n\nAnother consideration: `_parse_json` may call `transform_source=None` \u2192 no transform. It uses `json.loads` with `object_pairs_hook`? In newer versions there's handling for `undefined` via `_JSON_OPERATORS`... For strict JSON input, fine.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `ie._real_extract(url)` for the course path needs `self._downloader` anywhere \u2014 `_parse_json` no, `_search_regex` no (match succeeds). Good.\n\nWhat about `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 classmethod, no instance needed. Good.\n\nNow, let me also handle the possibility that `_VALID_URL` requires specific URL shapes. I'll fetch it.\n\nLet me also double-check `mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')` \u2014 for the course URL, chapter_id and lesson_id are None; course_id is the captured group. For single video URL, course_id None.\n\nOK \u2014 let me make the get_info calls now.\n\nActually, one more thing: the test file should be self-contained and runnable. The module path: `youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy`. The snippet shows the class defined with those imports \u2014 consistent with the real module.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I worry that `LinuxAcademyIE` might not be exported? Direct module import works.\n\nLet me now write the get_info calls.\n\nAfter that, finalize.\n\nOne more design decision: instead of patching class methods with monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, ...), I could create the instance and patch instance attributes. Class-level patching with monkeypatch restores cleanly (uses __dict__). I'll go class-level.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety with class-level patching and `monkeypatch.setattr`: for classes, `oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)`; the original `_download_webpage` is defined in `InfoExtractor` (parent), not in `LinuxAcademyIE.__dict__`! So `LinuxAcademyIE.__dict__.get('_download_webpage')` \u2192 notset \u2192 with raising=True \u2192 AttributeError!\n\nHmm! That's a problem. `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake, raising=True)` would raise AttributeError because LinuxAcademyIE doesn't define `_download_webpage` in its own `__dict__` (it inherits from InfoExtractor).\n\nWait, let me re-check pytest's monkeypatch.setattr:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(\"%r object has no attribute %r\" % (target, name))\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nSo `oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)` \u2014 for the class, getattr finds the inherited method \u2192 not notset \u2192 no raise. Then since target is a class, oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset) \u2192 notset (since it's inherited). Then undo: `if value is not notset: setattr(obj, name, value) else: delattr(obj, name)` \u2014 value is notset \u2192 `delattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage')` \u2192 deletes the class attribute that monkeypatch itself set \u2192 restores inheritance. \n\nSo class-level patching works correctly: it sets the attribute on LinuxAcademyIE (shadowing the inherited one) and on undo deletes it, restoring the inherited method. No pollution. \n\nAlternatively, instance-level: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 oldval = getattr(ie, ...) \u2192 bound method (exists) \u2192 stored; undo: setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', bound_method) \u2192 leaves instance attribute pointing to bound method of that instance. Instance is discarded \u2192 no cross-test pollution. Also fine.\n\nI'll use class-level patching \u2014 cleaner semantics.\n\nHmm, but one concern with class-level patching: if tests run in parallel or the class is used elsewhere during the test \u2014 not an issue here.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether to patch `_sort_formats` or use the real one. Let me get_info on it to decide. If the real one works with simple dicts, using it adds a bit more realism. But its behavior with my fake format dicts: it sorts by preference fields; missing fields \u2192 treated as... Let me check the implementation via get_info.\n\nActually, you know, let me keep the fake to keep the test hermetic and focused. But I want to assert `_sort_formats` was called \u2014 my fake records that.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe better to let the real `_sort_formats` run to avoid patching more than needed? The risk: `_sort_formats` might raise on formats lacking required keys. Let me check its source via get_info and decide. If it's safe, I'll use the real one with well-formed fake formats (each with 'format_id', 'url', 'ext', 'preference'...). Actually the real `_sort_formats` in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats=None, field_preference=None):\n    if not field